  introduction: "Linear programming is an essential branch of applied mathematics that deals with optimizing a linear objective function subject to linear inequality or equality constraints. In this chapter, we master graphical modeling, bounded and unbounded feasible regions, the corner point method, multiple optimal solution line segments, and real-world industrial applications including manufacturing production scheduling, diet formulation, resource allocation, and transportation networks.",
  summary: [
    "Linear Programming Problem (LPP): Optimizes a linear objective function Z = ax + by subject to linear resource constraints and non-negativity restrictions x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0.",
    "Feasible Region (Convex Set): The common region satisfying all constraints simultaneously. The line segment joining any two points in the region lies entirely inside it.",
    "Fundamental Theorem 1 (Extreme Point Theorem): The optimal (maximum or minimum) value of Z must occur at a corner point (vertex) of the feasible region.",
    "Fundamental Theorem 2 (Bounded Region): A bounded feasible region always guarantees the existence of both a maximum and a minimum value at corner points.",
    "Unbounded Feasible Region Test: For minimum m, test open half-plane ax + by < m. If it has no common points with the region, m is the minimum. For maximum M, test ax + by > M. If it has common points, Z has no maximum value.",
    "Multiple Optimal Solutions: If two adjacent corner points yield the same optimal value of Z, every point on the line segment connecting them also yields the same optimal value.",
    "Applied Formulations: Diet problems (minimizing cost subject to minimum nutritional units), manufacturing problems (maximizing profit subject to machine/labour hour limits), and transportation problems (minimizing shipping cost across multiple sources and destinations)."
],
